 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the real property law, in relation to directing the department\nof financial services to establish an internet website for federal home equity\nconversion mortgage product comparison",
    "basePrintNo": "S3221",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3221-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 7,
       "fullName": "Anna Kaplan",
       "imgName": "1044_Anna_Kaplan.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 1224,
       "sessionMemberId": 1222,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "KAPLAN"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §280-b, RP L",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S3221\n\nSPONSOR: SANDERS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the real property law, in relation to directing the\ndepartment of financial services to establish an internet website for\nfederal home equity conversion mortgage product comparison\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill would create a new website, run by the Department of Financial\nServices, to compare HUD home equity conversion mortgage information,\nrates, and features to seniors in order to create a buyer's market for\nthese products and ensure seniors have reliable, updated, and comprehen-\nsive information about the products.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Requires the Department of Financial Services to establish\nand operate a home equity conversion mortgage comparison website. The\nwebsite will provide information to seniors, including information and\ncomparisons on reverse mortgage loan products in order to help seniors\nmake educated decisions on which products to choose.  The website will\nallow the consumer to compare the cost, rate, and service features of\nthese products.\n\nLenders may submit their reverse mortgage products for comparison on the\nwebsite so long as they submit complete reverse mortgage loan pricing to\nthe department and all factors relating to the reverse mortgage loans\naffecting future rate adjustments. The prices and costs provided must be\nas low or lower than those offered via other avenues, and the lender\nmust pay a fee determined by DFS to maintain the website.\n\nSection 2: The act will take effect one hundred and eighty days after it\nbecomes a law, but DFS may immediately take whatever actions necessary\nto begin is implementation.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nUnder current law, there exists no state-run comparison website for home\nequity conversion mortgage products.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nReverse mortgages are complicated and expensive financial products.\nMany seniors do not understand how they work or what their true longterm\ncosts are. Exacerbating this problem are unscrupulous lenders who market\nreverse mortgages as public services or government-sponsored products.\nInadequate regulation of this industry resulted in a sharp uptick in\ndefaults in 2016, as more seniors fell into foreclosure on these\nproducts, losing not only their homes, but also their most significant\nfinancial assets.\n\nForeclosures in the reverse mortgage industry have taken place against\nseniors for making payments mere cents short of their tax or mortgage\ninsurance bills. Lenders eager to tap the equity in these homes are\nsometimes aggressive to foreclose and see a return on their investment.\nSeniors can be better protected by providing them with clearer, more\naccurate information about these products and helping seniors to compare\nthese products and their associated costs.\n\nA reverse mortgage comparison website monitored and run by DFS would\ncreate a buyer's market that would empower seniors to seek out lenders\nand compare products offered by various lenders in order to make and\neducated decision absent any form of high pressure marketing or\nsubjective/selective information provided by potentially unscrupulous\nlenders.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nLOCAL FISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
